# Lesson 09 of the Plasm Koans. Each row is one koan: what the learner discovers,
# and the acceptance scenario of Plasm it teaches (feature: scenario).
Feature: About aggregates and references
  An aggregate is a record with the records it owns, read whole: an Order is always read with its lines, in one more command for any number of orders. Other aggregates are referred to by id and reached with a query, a join or an exists; binding them to each record with a let! comes after joins.

  Prerequisites: lesson 08.

  Background:
    Given the koans database with its starting data

  Scenario Outline: <koan>
    Given the koan "<koan>" in this lesson
    Then it passes with its reference answer and fails until its blanks are filled
    And it teaches the acceptance scenario "<teaches>"

    Examples:
      | koan                                                    | the learner discovers                                                                                                                                                                        | teaches                                                                                      | hint                                                                                                                                                                                 |
      | An order is read with its lines                         | an Order owns its lines: reading an order always reads its Lines, a plain list                                                                                                               | owned-collections: Reading an invoice reads its lines                                        | Count the rows of order_lines under Given whose order_id is the first order's.                                                                                                       |
      | Lines come in the order of their key                    | owned records are read in the order of their key unless [<OrderedBy>] names a field                                                                                                          | owned-collections: Reading an invoice reads its lines                                        | Read the ids of the first order's rows in order_lines under Given, smallest first, each wrapped as an OrderLineId.                                                                   |
      | No lines is an empty list                               | an order without lines reads with []: an owned list is never left unread                                                                                                                     | owned-collections: An invoice without lines reads with an empty list                         | The comment says what a read gives for an order that has no lines.                                                                                                                   |
      | Every order's lines come in one more command            | four orders and all their lines take two commands, and reading Lines sends nothing                                                                                                           | owned-collections: Every invoice's lines are read in one more command                        | The comment counts the commands: the read of the orders, and the read of every order's lines.                                                                                        |
      | A page of orders reads only its lines                   | with take 2, only the lines of those two orders are read                                                                                                                                     | elegance: Database work remains bounded by the requested page                                | Count the rows of order_lines under Given that belong to the orders the take keeps, then the commands as in the previous koan.                                                       |
      | A customer is referred to by id                         | an Order holds its customer's CustomerId, a reference to tableOf<Customer>; a query reaches the customer                                                                                     | constraints: A typed id references the stored record whose key it is                                | Read customer_id in the third row of the orders table under Given, as the option of a CustomerId, then find that id in the customers table.                                          |
      | A join reads both aggregates' fields                    | a join pairs orders with their customers in one command; an order without a customer is left out                                                                                             | joins: An inner join returns matching pairs                                                  | For each row of the orders table under Given that has a customer_id, pair the order's id with that customer's name, in id order.                                                     |
      | A whole order from a join comes with its lines          | a whole Order selected from a join is read with its lines, in one more command                                                                                                               | owned-collections: A whole Invoice selected from a join comes with its lines                 | The first row is Ada's oldest order, so count its rows in order_lines under Given, and the comment counts the commands.                                                              |
      | Filtering orders by their lines                         | List.exists over o.Lines is run by the database, and keeps each matching order once                                                                                                          | linked-collections: Cards are kept by their tags, as F# reads a list                         | Find the rows of order_lines under Given whose product_id is the Gadget's, and list their order ids once each, wrapped as OrderIds.                                                  |
      | Every line passes, as F# reads it                       | List.forall over o.Lines keeps an order with no lines, as F# would                                                                                                                           | linked-collections: Cards whose every tag passes, as F# reads a list                         | Keep each order that has no order_lines row priced under ten, which, as the comment says, includes an order with no lines at all.                                                    |
      | Orders with a succeeded payment                         | exists (an inner query over referring<Payment> o) reaches another aggregate inside a where                                                                                                   | query-exists: Orders with a succeeded payment                                                | List the order_id of each succeeded row of the payments table under Given, once each and in order.                                                                                   |
      | Orders without a succeeded payment                      | not (exists ...) keeps the orders no matching payment refers to                                                                                                                              | query-exists: Orders without a succeeded payment                                             | These are the orders that the previous koan left out.                                                                                                                                |
